  "notes": "West leads the ♥K and continues the suit. After ruffing the second heart lead, South's only possible loser is in spades. South must try to prevent the defense from scoring a trick with the ♠K. South should not play the ♠A. That plan would succeed only if one of the defenders held the singleton King of spades.\n\nInstead, South should cross to dummy and play a spade towards the closed hand. If East follows low, South should play the ♠Q (or any other spade besides the ♠A), taking a finesse. When the finesse wins, South can return to the dummy and finesse again. If East still has another spade left the ♠A will take care of it.\n\nAs South needs two dummy entries, he must be careful how he plays the diamonds. If South plays the ♦2 to the ♦K, he will have blocked the suit and be unable to return to the dummy.\n\nSouth can succeed by leading his ♦2 to dummy's ♦10 or ♦9 the first time, finesse spades, and subsequently overtake his ♦Q or ♦J with dummy's ♦K.\n\nAlternatively, South could overtake his ♦Q or ♦J the first time and, after finessing in spades, lead the ♦2 to dummy's ♦10 or ♦9.",
